findings.body:
**Credentialed CORS now allows any origin.** Returning `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` set to the request `origin` (or `*`) together with `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true` means any malicious site can make credentialed cross-origin requests and read the responses — session cookies and authenticated API data are exposed to attacker-controlled pages.

Native mobile clients are outside browser CORS; they do not need a reflected origin. Webviews that send `Origin` still need an explicit allowlist (known https hosts / app schemes), not an open reflect.

Also, `Origin: *` + `Credentials: true` (when `origin` is null) is invalid per the CORS spec and browsers will ignore it.

Restore an allowlist (include any new mobile webview origins), and only attach `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials` when the origin matched:

```suggestion
  const ALLOWED = new Set([
    "https://app.example.com",
    // add known mobile webview origins here
  ]);
  if (origin && ALLOWED.has(origin)) {
    return {
      "Access-Control-Allow-Origin": origin,
      "Access-Control-Allow-Credentials": "true",
      Vary: "Origin",
    };
  }
  return { Vary: "Origin" };
```
